## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model simple state transitions and undo/redo semantics with basic data structures.

## Key Concepts

- History management with stacks
- Amortized O(1) operations
- Edge cases: empty history, initial page

## Code Example

```javascript
class BrowserHistory {
  constructor(startUrl) {
    this.back = [];
    this.forward = [];
    this.currentUrl = startUrl ?? null;
  }
  visit(url) {
    if (this.currentUrl !== null) this.back.push(this.currentUrl);
    this.forward = [];
    this.currentUrl = url;
  }
  back(steps) {
    while (steps > 0 && this.back.length) {
      this.forward.push(this.currentUrl);
      this.currentUrl = this.back.pop();
      steps--;
    }
    return this.currentUrl;
  }
  forward(steps) {
    while (steps > 0 && this.forward.length) {
      this.back.push(this.currentUrl);
      this.currentUrl = this.forward.pop();
      steps--;
    }
    return this.currentUrl;
  }
  current() {
    return this.currentUrl;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale to multiple tabs with independent histories?
- How would you extend to persist history across sessions without refetching pages?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-time analytics require fast percentile queries with inserts/deletes while handling duplicates.

## Key Concepts
- Coordinate compression maps latencies to a compact domain.
- Fenwick tree maintains prefix counts efficiently.
- kth-order statistic via binary lifting yields the percentile value.
- Carefully handle erase when a value count reaches zero.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.bit=new Array(n+1).fill(0); }
  update(i, delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+= i&-i) this.bit[i]+=delta; }
  query(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-= i&-i) s+=this.bit[i]; return s; }
  kth(k){ let idx=0; let bit=1; while((bit<<1) <= this.n) bit<<=1; for(; bit>0; bit>>=1){ let nxt = idx + bit; if(nxt <= this.n && this.bit[nxt] < k){ idx = nxt; k -= this.bit[nxt]; } } return idx+1; }
}
```

